# cvvdp video vs fast-ssim2 per-frame — deterministic-band parallelism
#
# Re-run of the 2026-09-24 comparison after cvvdp's video pipeline gained
# dimension-governed plane banding (`src/par.rs`, modelled on fast-ssim2's
# `Tuning`): band boundaries are a pure function of plane length — never
# of RAYON_NUM_THREADS — so ST and MT execute the identical partition.
# Elementwise maps are bit-identical by construction; reduce kernels fold
# per-band partials in fixed band order (deterministic; ulp-level
# re-association vs the old flat accumulation, inside the 1e-3 JOD
# parity gate — scores in this file are identical to the pre-banding
# runs at the printed 1e-6 precision).
#
# Banded under feature `parallel` (default-on):
#   - emit: all per-level elementwise ops (sensitivities, weber scaling,
#     masking min/pow/diff chains) + raw-partial reductions (lp2, xcm4)
#   - masking: σ=3 13-tap separable blur (row bands both passes; V-pass
#     reads the full H-pass plane by absolute row for its halo)
#   - convert: DKL conversion (u8/u16/f32, interleaved+planar)
#   - gauss_l: both shared pyramid builds under rayon::join
#   - PAR_MIN_SAMPLES = 1<<17 — smaller planes stay serial (dispatch
#     overhead dominates below ~128k samples; ssim2 uses 1<<18).
#
# FIR additionally switched to a FUSED N-tap kernel (`vfir_into`/
# `vfir2_into` in simd_math): each output channel is one pass over all
# fl window-slot planes — every source plane read once per output
# element (~3x less plane traffic than the per-tap vscale+vaxpy chain;
# the stage is memory-bound, so this beat threading — 57.6 -> 24.6 ms/f
# @1080p 8t, and 56.4 -> 28.5 at 1t). Per-element add order is the same
# k-ascending sequence → bit-identical. low_memory keeps the per-tap
# chain (its source planes are materialised one slot at a time).
#
# Second pass on the same file (later on 2026-09-24): four more
# traffic cuts on top of the banding — all bit-identical:
#   - FIR writes straight into each channel's Gaussian band-0 slot;
#     the `filt` staging planes and the band-0 copies are gone
#     (~128 MB of copy traffic per 1080p frame, −64 MB RSS).
#   - Shared sustained-A expands: every channel divides by the same
#     `l_bkg` pyramid, so `expand(gauss_l[k+1])` is now computed once
#     per level per side (`weber_bands_from_gauss_lexp`) instead of
#     once per channel — 24 identical expands per side → 6.
#   - Four-channel CSF sensitivity fusion (`compute_sensitivities4_
#     slice`): one pass shares the log_l load + LUT bracket math
#     across all four channel maps.
#   - Masking `|T−R|` + `safe_pow` fused into one pass
#     (`vabs_diff_pow_into`); the raw-diff plane is gone.
#
# Third pass (same day, after the fcvvdp comparison — see
# video_vs_fcvvdp_2026-09-24.*): the pyramid passes (reduce/expand
# V+H) and vweber bands now split their own output rows internally,
# and the emit's channel walk is serial over the banded kernels —
# the 8-way channel×side scope (uneven task tail) is gone. The walk
# shares two PyramidScratch sets instead of eight per-cache
# scratches (−66 MB RSS at 1080p; cvvdp-lm also benefits,
# 614→531 MB). All bit-identical — only scheduling moved.
#
# Host:        lilith (water-cooled AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 49 GiB RAM)
# Kernel:      Linux 7.0.0-31-generic
# Rust:        rustc 1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01)
# Build:       cargo build -p cvvdp --release --example video_vs_ssim2
#              (workspace target/, release profile, no target-cpu=native)
# Repo:        zenmetrics @ 4d4e0bef (master@origin) + this change
# fast-ssim2:  ../fast-ssim2/fast-ssim2 @ f011259 (v0.9.0-15-gf011259)
#
# Corpus:      deterministic synthetic clip generated inside the harness —
#              same frames for both metrics; `gen` rows measure synthesis
#              cost alone (ms/frame claims subtract it: (wall-gen)/24).
#              24 frames @ 30 fps, median of 3 reps.
#
# Replay (per cell — metric in {gen,ssim2,cvvdp,cvvdp-lm}):
#   RAYON_NUM_THREADS=<1|8> target/release/examples/video_vs_ssim2 \
#       <metric> <W> <H> 24 30 3
#
# Headline deltas vs the 2026-09-24 first pass (1080p, (wall-gen)/24):
#   cvvdp 1t: 281.4 -> 213.0 ms/f   (CPU 281.7 -> 211.9, -25%)
#   cvvdp 8t: 160.1 -> 129.6 ms/f   (CPU 935.4 -> 793.3, -15%)
#   cvvdp-lm 8t: 186.2 -> 164.7 ms/f
#   ssim2 8t (this run): 144.2 ms/f (runs range 130.6-144.2)
#   => cvvdp 8t wall now BEATS ssim2 at 720p/1080p (53.8 vs 63.7;
#      129.6 vs 144.2); ssim2 still leads at 512^2 (15.1 vs 19.7 —
#      cvvdp's 4-channel constant dominates small planes).
#   => Serial CPU is within ~1.14x of ssim2 at 1080p (211.9 vs
#      186.7). 8t user+sys is ~2.7x (793.3 vs 290.8): cvvdp computes
#      strictly more per frame and the parallel stages are
#      bandwidth-bound, so contention inflates CPU per frame.
